  "prediction": "**CBS News Overhauls \"The Early Show\" with New Team**\n\nIn a bid to boost ratings, CBS News has given its morning show \"The Early Show\" a complete makeover, replacing co-hosts Harry Smith and Maggie Rodriguez with Chris Wragge and Erica Hill, the current Saturday anchor team. The new team will start on January 3, 2024.\n\nHarry Smith, who had the longest tenure on the show, will stay at CBS as a news reporter and primary substitute anchor for the \"CBS Evening News,\" \"Face the Nation,\" and \"Sunday Morning.\" Smith expressed gratitude for his opportunities and wished the new team the best in moving the ratings needle.\n\nThe new team includes Chris Wragge, who worked in sports before joining news in 2006, and Erica Hill, who came from CNN and has been reading newscasts on the current daily program. Jeff Glor, who has been anchoring the network's Saturday newscast, will join the team as the regular news reader. Marysol Castro, formerly of ABC's \"Good Morning America\" weekend edition, will serve as the new weather anchor.\n\nThe changes come after the show consistently ranked behind NBC's \"Today\" show and ABC's \"Good Morning America\" in the network morning news ratings. CBS News President Sean McManus believes the new team will help the show grow, citing their chemistry and ability to shift between hard news and lighter fare.\n\nVeteran television critic David Bianculli praised CBS for looking to shake things up, but noted that the suddenness of the changes may smell of desperation. Rodriguez and Price, who are leaving the show, are discussing future roles at the network.\n\nThe new team's success will be closely watched, as \"The Early Show\" aims to break out of third place in the ratings.",
  "answers": [
    "CBS is scrapping its Early Show team, replacing co-hosts Harry Smith and Maggie Rodriguez with the anchors of the show's Saturday edition, Chris Wragge and Erica Hill. Other more minor players also are leaving in the switch, which takes place early next year, the LA Times reports. The show has been consistently stuck in third place, well behind Today and Good Morning America. Smith, who survived the last major overhaul of the show in 2002, will stick with the network as Katie Couric's main backup on the CBS Evening News, notes AP. Click here for more."
